  "summary": {
    "en": {
      "caveats": "The study relies on Medicaid claims from a single state during the early pandemic period, which may reflect unique healthcare utilization dynamics and policy waivers.",
      "what": "Analysis of 2020-2021 Medicaid claims across 424 ZIP Code Tabulation Areas in South Carolina demonstrates that predicted in-person prenatal accessibility showed no significant urban-rural difference, yet observed utilization was significantly lower in rural areas (p < .001). Telehealth accessibility remained heavily skewed toward urban areas, with zero predicted telehealth access recorded in 36.76% of rural areas compared to 7.53% of urban ones. Researchers combined broadband availability constraints with floating catchment area methods and 30-minute visit volumes, assessing spatial associations through spatial lag and error models.",
      "why": "The findings counter the assumption that virtual care automatically overcomes geographic health barriers, demonstrating that telehealth operates within and mirrors existing in-person obstetric deficits rather than evening them out."
    }
  },
